Thus, the probability that a randomly chosen side of length less than 1 is acute is equal to the area of the regular triangle of side length 1 divided by the area of the unit circle, which is $\frac{\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}}{π} = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4\pi}$.  Thus, the probability that we get an obtuse triangle is $1 - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4\pi}$, so $4p = 4\left(1 - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4\pi}\right) = \boxed{4 - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{\pi}}$. </think> </answer>

